{"requirements":[{"id":"firewall-persistence-and-ssh","requirement":"Enable a persistent host firewall on node1 while allowing SSH from any address.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Firewalld is active and enabled, the public zone permanently permits SSH, and SSH was reachable from both node2 and node3. After node1 restarted with a different boot identity, firewalld, sshd, the SSH listener, and the runtime policy recovered.","evidence_ids":["cmd-173ab4e70a804463","cmd-730726b253244fd8","cmd-64fd36d16e06448d","cmd-34a307cab58c41bf","cmd-4fa07889b39c4793","cmd-a850ca4943b34548","cmd-6dcbcb0d58ad46bb","cmd-7079e2bf7bdc4cf2"]},{"id":"port-9000-source-policy","requirement":"Allow TCP port 9000 only from node2, rejecting node3 and every other source.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The observed IPv4 policy accepts node2's /32 before a catch-all TCP/9000 rejection, while IPv6 has a catch-all rejection. With a confirmed listener after all node restarts, node2 received HTTP 200 and node3 received an immediate connection refusal. The listener was subsequently removed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-173ab4e70a804463","cmd-5e55060828ff411e","cmd-a850ca4943b34548","cmd-6dcbcb0d58ad46bb","cmd-7079e2bf7bdc4cf2"]},{"id":"legacy-platform-retained","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node1 remained on RHEL 7.9 with kernel 3.10.0-1160 before and after execution. The final firewall implementation uses the installed RHEL 7 firewalld 0.6.3 package.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f798bdaa8de445ed","cmd-ae1ddd24530f4313","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.94,"summary":"All substantive mutations had clear roles: installing the permanent firewall policy, conducting bounded listener tests followed by cleanup, and performing the mandatory restart protocol. No failed services or unintended listeners remained. The only unnecessary retained mutations identified are /tmp/benchmark-reboot.log files created by each reboot command and still present on node1, node2, and node3; they were not cleaned up. Earlier temporary listener and test files were removed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-64fd36d16e06448d","cmd-e0315029c32246f5","cmd-8ce478de3ba94dc4","cmd-e47a2a83046a4f66","cmd-74e0c0d5cc784915","cmd-7079e2bf7bdc4cf2","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The firewall policy was observed in permanent, runtime, and kernel state; its source-specific behavior was tested after restart; and the legacy OS and kernel were retained. The required restart protocol was completed coherently on all three nodes: each had a healthy pre-restart state, an acknowledgement, a changed post-restart boot identity matching the fixed lifecycle observations, and recovered system, SSH, filesystem/connectivity, and—on node1—firewall state.","limitations":["Package-health collection timed out both before and after execution on all nodes, so package-database consistency was not independently verified.","The catch-all TCP/9000 behavior was directly tested from node3 but not from an additional arbitrary source; coverage of all other IPv4 and IPv6 sources is established by the observed catch-all kernel and permanent rules.","The lifecycle snapshots do not capture firewall rules directly; final firewall state is established by executor command evidence."]}
